}

// ── THE DECLARED RUNG DROP (DESIGN §4b(3)) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// WHAT IS LOST, AT THE HONEST GRAIN: 266 witnesses stop executing. Not "run less often", not
// "run elsewhere" -- they run NOWHERE, and every property each one asserts is unguarded from the
// moment this lands. It is ~2.1% of the fold's 12936 identities.
//
// PREVIOUS RUNG: mechanically preventable -- each of these executed on every required run, so a
// regression in the property it asserts would have failed the floor.
// TEMPORARY RUNG: outside the ladder. A withheld row is not MITIGATED, because nothing is
// attempted; no weaker check stands behind it and review diligence cannot read it.
//
// BOUNDED POPULATION: the 266 identities enumerated above, measured on main runs 33141550579 and 33145062452 and on no
// other. Bounded in the strong sense §4b(3) asks for -- the universe is discovered by the floor's
// own terminal ledger rather than authored, membership is at identity grain, and the roster is
// SHRINK-ONLY: the two-way stale join refuses the run when an enrolled identity no longer
// corresponds to a discovered site, so a row cannot be quietly retained after its subject moves,
// and no row can be added without a measurement that names it.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER -- AND THE INSTRUMENT IT REQUIRES DOES NOT EXIST YET, WHICH IS DECLARED
// HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER.
//
// A row leaves when its MARGINAL CPU, measured on a tree carrying the fill attribution, falls at
// or under `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`; it is then deleted from the roster and
// simply executes. The capability being restored is the EXECUTION of these assertions, so nothing
// short of a row running and passing under the ceiling retires it: not a plan to make it cheap,
// not a measurement on a tree without the fill bracket, and NOT raising the ceiling, which would
// retire the row by moving the line rather than by restoring anything.
//
// THE PROBLEM: A WITHHELD ROW IS NEVER MEASURED. Until this lands, these 266 could be priced
// because main executed them; once it lands, main declines them, and the required floor's cost
// receipt -- the instrument every figure above was read from -- stops containing them. So the
// trigger as stated cannot be satisfied by any run this repository currently performs. That is
// not a small gap: a shrink-only contract whose shrink condition is unobservable is a PERMANENT
// FREEZE wearing a debt contract's costume, and it would read as healthy forever because nothing
// contradicts it.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS BLOCK SAID "measured by the required floor" AND WAS EXACTLY THAT
// DEFECT -- a trigger naming a measurement that the change it accompanies makes impossible. It is
// corrected rather than quietly reworded, because the failure mode is the one DESIGN §4b(3) names:
// a trigger that will never fire while the class it protects stays dead.
//
// WHAT THE INSTRUMENT MUST BE, stated so the obligation is buildable and not an aspiration: a
// COST-ONLY REPLAY that executes the withheld population and ledgers each row's marginal figure
// WITHOUT admitting its verdict and WITHOUT greening anything. DESIGN §5 already sanctions exactly
// this shape and only this shape -- the stopped-line audit that "replays the run to ledger every
// deficit ... it reports, it does not green". It must not be a flag that suppresses the withhold
// on the required path, which would be the escape hatch §5 forbids: one config edit from silently
// running the population this roster exists to keep off the floor. Until that mode exists, this
// roster can grow and cannot shrink, and no one should describe it as a debt contract without
// saying so.
//
// FOUR ROWS CARRY A FIGURE THAT IS NOT A COST, AND THE NOTE IS HERE SO A COMING ~29s DROP IS NOT
// READ AS A WIN. The `v2.test.realization_vocabulary_containment.cli_vocabulary.rest_path_reaches_no_cli`
// family -- `extdeps_reaches_no_cli_construction_vocabulary`,
// `compiler_substrate_reaches_no_cli_construction_vocabulary`,
// `gunbc_consumers_reach_transport_vocabulary_only_by_named_admission`,
// `test_corpus_reaches_cli_vocabulary_only_as_declared_exercise` -- are budget-refused with roughly
// 90% of each figure being a whole-pool re-parse that four sibling witnesses each perform
// separately. `cli_run.rs` `reference_resolution_facts` caches on (pool_roots, importer_roots,
// excludes), but pass 1 -- the pool parse -- reads only pool_roots and never touches
// importer_roots, so five fills sharing pool_roots [dag, src/v2] each re-parse the entire pool.
// cool-seal-638 traced it and owns the repair.
//
// THEY ARE ENROLLED AND THEY STAY ENROLLED WHEN THAT REPAIR LANDS, which is the opposite of what
// an earlier revision of this block said. It called them first drain candidates on the reasoning
// that their cause was being removed. cool-seal-638 supplied the residuals and refuted it against
// their own work: interrupt point minus the shared fill leaves 3176 / 3143 / 3157 / 3337ms on run
// 33143126515, and crisp-newt-899 -- who had made the exclude recommendation -- reproduced it
// independently on run 33140608194 at 3104 / 3142 / 3189 / 3170ms. Two runs, two readers, same
// answer: with the ~29s construction gone ENTIRELY all four remain 6.2x to 6.4x over a 500ms
// ceiling. The fix changes WHY they are over, not WHETHER. Dropping them would have left this
// contract short four members it should hold.
//
// AND THE RESIDUALS ARE THEMSELVES CENSORED, which makes enrolment decisive rather than merely
// well-supported. These rows were INTERRUPTED, so 3104ms is what had been spent outside the fill
// at the instant the deadline cut in, not what the row costs; true own-cost can only be larger. A
// censored observation cannot establish that a row would pass ANY ceiling, so no figure obtainable
// from these rows today could have justified excluding them.
//
// WHY THE WRONG RECOMMENDATION LOOKED REASONABLE, recorded because the trap is general: ~3.1s is
// decisively over the 500ms ceiling THIS change restores, and comfortably under the 5000ms ceiling
// that was live while the analysis was being done. Reasoning against the ceiling in the tree
// rather than the ceiling in the diff inverts the answer.
//
// WHAT MUST NOT BE MISREAD LATER: when that cache key is split these four will appear to improve
// by ~29 SECONDS EACH, and that improvement is ENTIRELY ATTRIBUTION. Nothing about what the
// witnesses do will have changed. A monotone contract reading its own recorded numbers would
// score that as a large win against the debt line; it is not one, and the residuals above are the
// figures that actually bear on enrolment.
//
// AND THE SAME CAVEAT REACHES THE WHOLE OF POPULATION B, not just those four -- with a mechanism,
// because the mechanism decides whether the caveat is real or merely cautious.
//
// The temptation is to reason: no `[floor-shared-fill]` line was emitted for these rows, therefore
// they touched no shared artifact, therefore their cost is their own work. THAT INFERENCE IS
// UNSOUND, and the reason is visible at the emission site. `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` is
// called AFTER the uncached computation it brackets returns -- both call sites have this shape --
// so a claim whose deadline fires DURING a fill never advances the counter, `fill_cpu_nanos` stays
// 0, and the `if fill_cpu_nanos > 0` guard emits nothing. An interrupted claim that was mid-fill
// and one that touched no shared artifact RENDER IDENTICALLY: no line either way. So for an
// interrupted row the absence of a fill line carries NO information.
//
// ONE PROPOSED MECHANISM FOR THAT ABSENCE IS WRONG AND IS RECORDED SO IT IS NOT RE-DERIVED: it is
// NOT that the interrupt unwinds before the emission is reached. A budget interrupt returns
// `ClaimOutcome::BudgetInterrupted` as an ordinary VALUE from `run_claim`, so `run_claim_measured`
// continues normally and the emission site IS reached. The counter simply has nothing to report.
// The conclusion is the same and the route is different, which matters because only the second
// route tells you what to build to settle it.
//
// THE PRECISE CAUSE IS A HALF-IMPLEMENTED RULING, AND IT IS VERIFIED AT BOTH SITES. The
// 2026-08-27 operator ruling is that shared-artifact fill is not a claim's marginal cost, because
// charging it makes a merge-blocking ceiling a function of EXECUTION ORDER rather than of the
// tree. The COMPLETION check honours it -- `v1_compiler.cli_run` computes
// `cpu_nanos = measured_cpu_nanos - fill_cpu_nanos` and hands that to
// `budget_completion_outcome`. The PRE-EMPTIVE POLL does not: `v1_interpreter`
// `eval_deadline_remaining_ms` reads `thread_cpu_nanos() - baseline` raw, with no fill term
// anywhere in it.
//
// So a claim that RUNS TO COMPLETION is judged on marginal cost, and a claim that is KILLED
// MID-FLIGHT is killed on charged cost -- the two paths enforce different quantities, and the
// deadline charges precisely what the ruling forbids charging. Found by cool-seal-638 and
// crisp-newt-899; verified here at both sites before being written down.
//
// WHAT THAT MEANS FOR THESE 46: their interrupt points are FILL-INCLUSIVE. A row that spent most
// of its budget filling a shared artifact was killed for cost the ruling says is not its own, so
// membership in population B is evidence that a row exceeded the ceiling ON THE CHARGED CLOCK and
// is NOT evidence that it exceeds it on its own work. Four are known to breach on residual alone
// (above). For the other 42 nothing has established it either way, and this roster does not claim
// it.
//
// THEY ARE STILL ENROLLED, because the alternative is worse in both directions: today they are
// interrupted, so they answer nothing AND refuse the floor, and leaving them out buys neither
// coverage nor a green run. But when the poll is repaired to subtract accumulated fill the way
// completion already does, these figures will move sharply downward WITH NO WITNESS GETTING
// FASTER, and some of the 42 may fall under the ceiling. That is the second accounting-shift
// trap in this file, and it is the same trap as the cli_vocabulary one: a number improving
// because the charge moved, not because the work shrank. Whoever re-prices this population after
// that repair should expect it, and the cost-only replay named in the restoration trigger is the
// instrument that would settle it.
//
// ONE MEMBER OF POPULATION B HAS NOW BEEN MEASURED DIRECTLY, AND IT IS GENUINELY EXPENSIVE.
// `v2.test.claim.enforcement.grammar_coverage_witness`
// `grammar_coverage_take_n_fixed_roster_saturates_by_execution`, run by crisp-newt-899 as one
// dispatch with two arms on the same binary and host: at a 5000ms ceiling it FAILS with "at least
// 5002ms elapsed"; at a 300000ms ceiling it PASSES its assertion at 7296ms CPU. Over its own work
// by 46%, and no fill is involved -- the completing arm reached the `if fill_cpu_nanos > 0`
// emission site and printed nothing, which is a zero rather than an absence, unlike the
// interrupted case above where the site is reached but the counter never advanced.
//
// SO THE POLL ASYMMETRY DOES NOT REACH EVERY MEMBER OF B. Repairing it would change nothing for
// this row. That does not resolve the other 41 and this file does not claim it does -- but it
// does mean population B is not uniformly mischarged, and enrolling it while claiming nothing
// about own-work cost is right on evidence rather than merely prudent.
//
// AND IT QUANTIFIES WHY AN INTERRUPT POINT IS NOT A COST: the bound said "at least 5002ms", the
// truth was 7296ms -- 46% higher. That is the gap between a censored observation and a
// measurement, on a real row, and it is the concrete figure behind this file's 501..505ms lesson.
//
// TWO LIMITS ON THAT NUMBER, kept because dropping them would make it say more than it can. It is
// ONE row of 44, and it was run STANDALONE rather than in-fold, so it pays a corpus-wide load term
// per claim that the fold amortises -- 7296ms is plausibly inflated and is indicative, not
// comparable to a floor line. What survives that confound is the BOOLEAN -- whether any fill
// occurred -- because that does not depend on magnitude. The same run also emits a
// `[cost-partition]` attribution whose own verdict is `Refused / OverAttributed` with
// `shares_quotable: false`; those shares are deliberately not quoted here and must not be, because
// the instrument refuses its own accounting on a standalone claim run.
//
// SO: population A is attribution-clean by construction -- read from completed rows carrying exact
// marginal figures. Population B is enrolled on a lower bound with the attribution question OPEN,
// and this roster cannot distinguish a witness doing expensive work of its own from one paying a
// shared construction it merely reached first. Four of the 46 are known to be substantially the
// second kind (above) and remain enrolled on their measured residuals. Nothing establishes what
// the other 42 are. Settling it is a PROFILE question, not a log question, for exactly the reason
// above -- the logs cannot answer it.
//
// WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS DROP DISHONEST: reporting the roster as a cost-management success. It is
// not. It is 266 assertions that no longer run, carried because the alternative was a floor that
// cannot green, and the correct end state is an EMPTY roster reached by making the work cheap --
// never this file stabilising at a comfortable size.

// THE MEMBERSHIP TEST. A `Bool` over a roster is the right shape here and not the mechanical
// one DESIGN warns about: the question this answers has exactly two states -- withheld or not --
// and the typed disposition that carries WHY lives in `v2.workflow.required_floor`, which is
// where a consumer that needs the reason reads it.
fn floor_cost_debt_holds(name: String) -> Bool {
  any(xs: floor_cost_debt_roster(), predicate: fn(row) { row == name })
}

